adjudication.rationale:
In `packages/app-store/googlecalendar/lib/CalendarService.ts`, `refreshAccessToken` now does:

```ts
const res = await refreshOAuthTokens(...)
const token = res?.data;
googleCredentials.access_token = token.access_token;
```

But `refreshOAuthTokens` (new file `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/refreshOAuthTokens.ts`) returns a `fetch(...)` Response object when `APP_CREDENTIAL_SHARING_ENABLED && process.env.CALCOM_CREDENTIAL_SYNC_ENDPOINT && userId` is truthy. A standard Fetch `Response` does not have a `.data` property (you must call `await res.json()` / `await res.text()`). Therefore, in sync mode `token` will be `undefined`, and `token.access_token` will throw at runtime. This is a concrete, diff-verifiable bug introduced by the new abstraction.

In `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`, after parsing the token refresh response, the code explicitly does:

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

This means any refresh response that legitimately omits `refresh_token` (common for many OAuth providers on refresh) will have its `refresh_token` field replaced with the literal string `"refresh_token"`.

That becomes a real persistence/correctness issue in call sites that merge the parsed data back into stored credentials. For example in `office365calendar/lib/CalendarService.ts`:

```ts
const tokenResponse = parseRefreshTokenResponse(responseJson, refreshTokenResponseSchema);
o365AuthCredentials = { ...o365AuthCredentials, ...(tokenResponse.success && tokenResponse.data) };
await prisma.credential.update({ ... data: { key: o365AuthCredentials } })
```

If the refresh response lacks `refresh_token`, the merge overwrites the previously valid stored refresh token with the placeholder string, breaking future refreshes. So the finding describes a real, verifiable defect introduced by the diff.
